# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S11 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r3 has "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → infinite_regress ∨ first_mover"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "in the world some things are in motion" and "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S11: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "in the world some things are in motion" and "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" are true and "the series of movers each put in motion by another goes on to infinity" and "there is a first mover, put in motion by no other" are false. Is S11, "If that by which it is put in motion be itself put in motion, then this also must needs be put in motion by another, and that by another again.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r3 has "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ not_act_pot_same → ¬◇self_motion" and "motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual ∧ ¬self_motion → moved_by_another" and "some_motion → other_movers"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.3 have "moved_in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual ∧ not_act_pot_same → ¬◇self_motion" and "¬◇self_motion → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → other_movers" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → infinite_regress ∨ first_mover"; they differ when only "in the world some things are in motion", "nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion", "a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act", "motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality", "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality", "the same thing cannot be at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect", "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" and "there is some other (subsequent) mover" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r3 has "god"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.3 have "first_mover ∧ god"; they differ when only "there is God" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is God" is true and "there is a first mover, put in motion by no other" is false. Is the conclusion, "Therefore it is necessary to arrive at a first mover, put in motion by no other; and this everyone understands to be God.", true then?
